<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mali</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Bamako</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Landlocked West African republic independent from France in 1960, which has had coups, Tuareg rebellions and, since 2012, a Sahel insurgency.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a>, which became independent on 20 June 1960, Senegal withdrew and the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> was renamed the Republic of Mali. On 20 August 1960 it proclaimed independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keïta</a> became the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Keïta set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> around the Sudanese Union, took a socialist course and nationalised much of the economy. Moussa Traoré overthrew him in a bloodless coup on 19 November 1968 and ruled until March 1991, when a protest movement and an army coup ended his government. Mali held multi-party elections from 1992, and Alpha Oumar Konaré won the first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> rebellions occurred from 1962 to 1964, from 1990 to 1995, from 2007 to 2009 and again from 2012. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> persists, with as many as 200,000 people held in direct servitude.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In January 2012 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> rebellion in the north led to a coup in March and the declaration of Azawad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> intervened on 11 January 2013. Assimi Goita led a coup on 18 August 2020 and another in 2021. A 2023 constitution was approved in a referendum, and in 2025 all political parties were dissolved and Goita was granted a five-year renewable term. In 2025 the militant group JNIM blockaded fuel supplies to cities.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mali.jpg" alt="Mali" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mali.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bamako</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Modibo Keïta, Moussa Traoré, Alpha Oumar Konaré, Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta, Assimi Goita</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, a Christian minority, traditional African religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bambara and other national languages, with French as a working language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Mali</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=mali" aria-label="Mali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Mali lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4335 3927L4335 3927"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4335 3927h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4335 3927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4335 3927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bamako. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=mali">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
